            "Core temperature is held near <strong>37 °C</strong> by the hypothalamus through <strong>negative feedback</strong>: a sensor detects movement away from the set point and effectors oppose it — vasodilatation and sweating to lose heat, vasoconstriction, shivering and raised metabolism to conserve and make it. Normal oral temperature is about <strong>36.1 to 37.2 °C</strong>, lowest in the early morning and highest in the late afternoon; rectal readings run about 0.5 °C higher than oral and axillary about 0.5 °C lower; older adults run cooler and may have a serious infection with a 'normal' 37.4; infants lose heat fast through a large surface area and cannot shiver. <strong>Fever</strong> (pyrexia) is a raised set point in response to pyrogens — above 37.5 to 38 °C depending on site and policy — and is graded low (to 38.0), moderate (38.1–39.0), high (39.1–40.0) and hyperpyrexia (above 40.5, a medical emergency). <strong>Hyperthermia</strong> is a rise despite a normal set point, because heat gain exceeds loss — heat stroke, malignant hyperthermia — and antipyretics do not work for it. <strong>Hypothermia</strong> is a core temperature below 35 °C: the newborn in a cold labour ward, the old man on a concrete floor all night, the patient after prolonged surgery.",
