            "The <strong>pulse</strong> is the wave of pressure felt in an artery with each ventricular contraction, and it is assessed for <strong>rate</strong>, <strong>rhythm</strong>, <strong>volume</strong> and the condition of the vessel. The normal adult rate is <strong>60 to 100</strong> per minute; a newborn's is 120 to 160, a toddler's about 100 to 130, falling through childhood; athletes and sleepers run slower. <strong>Tachycardia</strong> above 100 follows fever (roughly ten beats for each degree), pain, anxiety, exercise, anaemia, hypovolaemia, hypoxia, hyperthyroidism and drugs such as salbutamol; <strong>bradycardia</strong> below 60 follows athletic training, sleep, hypothermia, hypothyroidism, raised intracranial pressure, heart block and drugs such as digoxin and beta-blockers. The <strong>rhythm</strong> is regular, regularly irregular or irregularly irregular — the last is atrial fibrillation until proved otherwise — and an irregular pulse is counted for a <strong>full minute</strong>, never fifteen seconds multiplied by four. <strong>Volume</strong> is full and bounding in fever and fluid overload, weak and thready in shock. The radial pulse is counted with two or three fingers, never the thumb, which has a pulse of its own; the <strong>apical</strong> pulse is auscultated at the fifth left intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line for a full minute in infants, in irregular rhythms and before digoxin; an <strong>apex–radial deficit</strong> — apex faster than the wrist because weak beats do not reach it — is measured by two nurses counting simultaneously. The carotid is the pulse of the collapsed patient, the femoral of the shocked infant, the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial of the limb in a cast.",
