            "A health history is the organised account of a person's health, taken by interview and recorded as <strong>subjective data</strong>. It begins with <strong>biographical data</strong> — name, age, sex, address, occupation, religion, marital status, next of kin, language, NHIS status, who referred them — and the <strong>presenting complaint</strong> in the patient's own words: 'my stomach has been running for three days', not 'diarrhoea'. The complaint is then <strong>analysed</strong>, and the mnemonics exist to make sure nothing is missed: <strong>SOCRATES</strong> for a symptom — site, onset, character, radiation, associated symptoms, timing, exacerbating and relieving factors, severity — or <strong>PQRST</strong> for pain: provoking and palliating factors, quality, region and radiation, severity, timing. Then the <strong>past medical and surgical history</strong> (including hypertension, diabetes, sickle cell status, tuberculosis, HIV status if known, previous admissions, operations, transfusions and anaesthetic problems), the <strong>obstetric history</strong> in women (gravida, para, last menstrual period, contraception), <strong>medicines</strong> — prescribed, bought, herbal and the ones a relative brought — and <strong>allergies</strong>, with the reaction described, because 'allergic to penicillin' that turns out to be mild nausea is a different fact from anaphylaxis.",
