            "The physical examination gathers <strong>objective data</strong> through four techniques used in a fixed order — <strong>inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation</strong> — because each one, done early, changes what the next would have found. <strong>Inspection</strong> is deliberate looking, and it starts the moment the patient walks or is wheeled in: general appearance, apparent age, build and nutrition, posture and gait, distress, colour (pallor in the conjunctivae, palms and nail beds; cyanosis of the lips and tongue — central — or of the fingers — peripheral; jaundice in the sclerae in daylight), breathing pattern, hygiene and dress, odour (ketones, alcohol, uraemia, infection), the mental state and the tone of voice. Good light, a warm room, adequate exposure of the part being examined with the rest covered, and a comparison of one side with the other are its tools. <strong>Palpation</strong> uses warm, clean hands: the fingertips for texture, pulsation, size and tenderness; the back of the fingers for temperature; the palm for vibration; light palpation (about 1 cm) before deep (4–5 cm); the tender area last, or the patient guards and the rest of the examination is lost. <strong>Percussion</strong> taps the middle finger of one hand laid flat on the body with the tip of the other middle finger and listens to the note — resonant over air-filled lung, hyper-resonant over a pneumothorax or emphysema, dull over the liver, a full bladder or consolidated lung, stony dull over a pleural effusion, tympanitic over a gas-filled gut. <strong>Auscultation</strong> uses the diaphragm for high-pitched sounds (breath sounds, bowel sounds, normal heart sounds) and the bell for low (murmurs, bruits), always on bare skin in a quiet room, never through clothing.",
